	<ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="tb-00" contextRef="c-00">
&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 1 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Company Structure and Nature of Operations&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USG Pipeline Company, LLC ("USGPC") is a wholly owned subsidiary of USG Corporation. USGPC converted from a corporation to an LLC in 2011.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC operates approximately 15 miles of 10" interstate pipeline, which was built to transport natural gas from a single interconnection with an interstate pipeline in Marion County, Tennessee to Jackson County, Alabama. USGPC only provides transportation services. It has no storage, compression, gathering or production facilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC was initially certificated, in 1997, in order to serve a new gypsum wallboard plant which was constructed by its affiliate, United States Gypsum Company ("USGypsum"). Although it has offered open-access services to the public since 1999, USGPC performed transportation service for only USGypsum prior to 2012. It did so pursuant to a Part 157 transportation agreement contained in Volume 2 of its tariff, which prescribed charges different from USGPC's open access rates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;Since early 2012, USGPC has provided Firm Transportation ("FT") and Interruptible Transportation ("IT") transportation service pursuant to its open access transportation tariff. In early 2012, USGPC added a delivery point (also in Jackson County, Alabama) for a new customer, The Cooperative District for Northeast Alabama Gas ("CDNAG"), which agreed to reimburse USGPC for costs incurred in connection with the new CDNAG delivery point. To address this operational change, USGPC modified its receipt point and also added a meter at its delivery point to its affiliate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;In order to free up firm capacity for CDNAG, USGypsum converted from its Part 157 service to an open access transportation agreement in February 2012, whereupon USGPC eliminated Volume 2 of its tariff. USGypsum executed FT and IT agreements at the maximum rates. CDNAG executed a long-term firm agreement at a negotiated rate and an IT agreement at the maximum rate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC is operated by personnel of its affiliate USGypsum and its parent, USG Corporation. USGPC has no dedicated personnel of its own. USGPC has a cash management agreement with USG Corporation, which was filed with the Federal Energy Regulatory Committee ("FERC") in 2012.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 2 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Summary of Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Basis of Accounting &lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC's accounting is subject to the requirements of the FERC. Since 2012, USGPC has maintained its accounts according to the Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America. Prior to that time, USGPC had a waiver of the Uniform System of Accounts and, except for a limited Form 2 (page 520) for 2011; it was not obligated to prepare and file a Form 2 for any period prior to 2012. There are no significant differences between regulatory accounting treatment and accounting treatment under accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America (GAAP) presented in these statements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;The preparation of financial statements in accordance with the FERC accounting requirements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the amounts reported in the financial statements and accompanying notes. These estimates include, but are not limited to, long-lived asset impairment, the effects of regulation, the effects of contingencies, etc. The estimates and assumptions used are based upon management's evaluation of the relevant facts and circumstances as of the date of the financial statements. Actual results could differ from those estimates. On an ongoing basis, management reviews estimates based on currently available information. Changes in facts and circumstances may result in revised estimates&lt;b&gt;.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Cash Management&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC has a cash management agreement with USG Corporation, which was filed with the FERC in 2012. The agreement establishes the rights and obligations under which USGPC will advance available, net cash funds resulting from its business activities to USG Corporation, either directly or through a wholly-owned affiliate(s) as may be designated by either party from time to time to perform actions consistent with the cash management agreement, and USG Corporation will provide cash management services for all funds so advanced.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;Property, plant and equipment is recorded at original cost.&amp;#xa0;Depreciation and amortization expense is calculated using a straight-line method.&amp;#xa0;Since it commenced open-access transportation services in 2012, USGPC has used a 5.0% depreciation rate for all of its facilities consistent with its applicable open-access rates.&amp;#xa0;Property, plant and equipment is tested for impairment whenever events or circumstances indicate that its carrying value may not be recoverable.&amp;#xa0;There were no impairments recorded for the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC recognizes at fair value legal obligations associated with the retirement of tangible long-lived assets that result from the acquisition, construction, or development, and/or normal operation of the assets. A liability is recorded for these obligations as long as the fair value can be reasonably estimated, even if the timing or method of settling the obligation is unknown. The asset retirement obligations are accreted using a credit-adjusted risk-free interest rate commensurate with the expected settlement dates of the asset retirement obligations. This rate is determined at the date the obligation is incurred. The associated retirement costs are capitalized as part of the related long-lived assets and are depreciated over the useful lives of the assets. Subsequent changes resulting from revisions to the timing or the amount of the original estimate of undiscounted cash flows are recognized as an increase or decrease in the carrying amount of the liability and the associated retirement cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;Changes in USGPC's liability for asset retirement obligations during 2025&amp;#xa0;and 2024&amp;#xa0;consisted of the following:&lt;/p&gt;

