&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USG Corporation and its consolidated subsidiaries are party to an agreement (Tax Sharing Agreement) that provides for the allocation of consolidated tax liabilities and benefits. The Tax Sharing Agreement generally provides that each party is allocated an amount of tax similar to that which would be owed had the party been separately subject to tax. Any net benefit attributable to the parent is reallocated to other members. USGPC is a disregarded entity for U.S. federal income tax purposes and the results of its operations are reported as a component of USG Corporation. There is no Tax Sharing Agreement that governs the allocation of cash payments and receipts between USG Corporation and its disregarded group members, any difference between the cash flows paid or received and the allocation method used for financial reporting purposes is reported in the separate financial statements of USGPC as a contribution to the capital of the party receiving the benefit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC recorded a benefit of ($454,387) and ($327,577) to income tax benefit and corresponding dividend during the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024, respectively, to reflect the effects of intercompany tax sharing that will not be settled via a Tax Sharing Agreement. The cumulative impact to retained earnings and corresponding dividends to reflect intercompany tax allocations that will not be settled via a Tax Sharing Agreement total $4,776,720 and $4,322,333 as of December 31, 2025 and 2024, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note 5 &lt;/b&gt;-&lt;b&gt; Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Legal Proceedings&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;In the normal course of its business, USGPC may be named as defendant in various legal proceedings. USGPC accrues a liability related to such matters when an obligation becomes probable and can be estimated. Management is not aware of any legal matters that would have a material impact on USGPC's financial position, results of operations, or cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Environmental Matters&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;text-align:justify;margin:0pt;"&gt;The operations of USGPC are subject to extensive and evolving federal, state and local environmental laws and regulations intended to protect the public health and the environment. Such environmental laws and regulations affect operations as they relate to impacts on air, water and land. USGPC had no liabilities required to be recorded for such matters as of December 31, 2025 or 2024.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;
</ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
	<ferc:GeneralInformationOnPlantAndOperationsTextblock id="tb-01" contextRef="c-00">
&lt;p style="margin: 0pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;, Times, serif; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;USGPC is an interstate pipeline that runs from a point of receipt in Marion County, Tennessee to two points of delivery in Jackson County, Alabama. All natural gas moves from north to south through a single 10-inch pipeline, which is approximately 15.5 miles long. USG Pipeline has no production, no gathering, no compression and no storage. Deliveries rely entirely on receipts and pressure from the sole upstream pipeline to which it is connected, East Tennessee Natural Gas, LLC.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="font-family:Times New Roman;font-size:10pt;font-variant:normal;margin:0pt;"&gt;Prior to 2012, USGPC's only customer was its affiliate, United States Gypsum Company, which was served under a Part 157 certificate. In 2012, USGPC connected to and commenced deliveries to an unaffiliated customer under its blanket certificate for construction and its blanket certificate for transportation. Service to United States Gypsum Company was converted to open-access service. Both delivery points are located close to each other in Alabama.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="margin: 0pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;, Times, serif; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;#xa0;&lt;/p&gt;
</ferc:GeneralInformationOnPlantAndOperationsTextblock>
